{:now [{:claim "A geometry is the theory of the invariants of a group acting on a space"
        :modern "A Klein geometry is a homogeneous space G/H: a Lie group G acting transitively on a manifold, H the stabiliser of a point; its properties are the G-invariant ones."
        :tex "(G, X),\\quad X \\cong G/H"}
       {:claim "Smaller group, more invariants"
        :modern "If H ⊂ G act on the same space, every G-invariant is H-invariant: the geometries form a lattice opposite to the lattice of subgroups. Euclidean ⊂ similarity ⊂ affine ⊂ projective on the plane has 3, 4, 6 and 8 parameters."
        :tex "E(2) \\subset \\mathrm{Sim}(2) \\subset \\mathrm{Aff}(2) \\subset \\mathrm{PGL}(3,\\mathbb{R})"}
       {:claim "The cross-ratio is the projective invariant"
        :modern "PGL(2) acts simply 3-transitively on the projective line, so four points have exactly one invariant, the cross-ratio; Cayley-Klein metrics are logarithms of it relative to a conic."
        :tex "(x_1,x_2;x_3,x_4) = \\frac{(x_3-x_1)(x_4-x_2)}{(x_4-x_1)(x_3-x_2)}"}]
 :fields [{:field :mechanics-physics :label "Physics" :how "Physical laws are classified by their symmetry groups: Galilean and Lorentz invariance, gauge groups; Noether ties each continuous symmetry to a conserved quantity." :example "Special relativity as the geometry of the Lorentz group (Minkowski 1908, explicitly after Klein)."}
          {:field :geometry :how "Cartan's generalised spaces (1920s) glue Klein geometries along a manifold; Thurston's eight 3-dimensional geometries are Klein geometries." :example "Cartan connections; Thurston's geometrisation conjecture, proved by Perelman 2003."}
          {:field :logic-computing :label "Computing" :how "Computer vision and graphics work in projective coordinates; invariants of a group are what a recogniser can rely on." :example "Homogeneous coordinates and 4x4 matrices in every 3D pipeline; cross-ratio invariants in camera-independent recognition."}
          {:field :abstract-algebra :how "Invariant theory of a group acting on polynomials, the 'theory of invariants' Klein named, became Hilbert's finiteness theorem and geometric invariant theory." :example "Hilbert's basis theorem (1890); Mumford's GIT (1965)."}]
